<p class="lead">
  A sideways PDF page is one of the most common — and most annoying — small formatting problems. It usually comes from a scanner, a phone camera app, or a fax gateway that got the orientation wrong. Fixing it takes seconds once you know where to look.
</p>

<h2>Fix it in your browser (no upload, free)</h2>
<p>
  The <a href="/tools/rotate-pdf/">Rotate PDF tool</a> on keptlocal rotates any page, or the whole document, without uploading the file anywhere.
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Drop your PDF onto the tool.</li>
  <li>Pick a rotation: 90° left, 180°, or 90° right.</li>
  <li>Choose <strong>All pages</strong>, or <strong>Specific pages</strong> and enter a range like <code>1, 3, 5-8</code>.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Rotate &amp; download</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p>
  Rotation is a page-object property in the PDF format, not a re-render — the underlying text and images are untouched, so there's no quality loss and no re-compression.
</p>

<h2>Why this happens</h2>
<p>
  Most sideways PDFs trace back to one of three sources. Flatbed and sheet-fed scanners record a fixed physical orientation regardless of how the paper was fed, so a document scanned in landscape shows up rotated when the software assumes portrait. Phone scanning apps sometimes bake in the camera's EXIF orientation incorrectly when converting a photo to PDF. And fax-to-PDF gateways, still common in legal and healthcare workflows, occasionally rotate pages during the format conversion.
</p>
<p>
  Whatever the cause, re-scanning the original document is almost never necessary — rotation is a metadata-level fix, not a content fix.
</p>

<h2>Other ways to rotate a PDF</h2>
<p>
  <strong>Adobe Acrobat</strong> (paid): Tools → Organize Pages → select pages → Rotate. Gives the same result with a more involved interface, useful if you're already inside Acrobat for other edits.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Your PDF reader's print dialog</strong>: Some readers let you set orientation at print time without changing the underlying file — fine for a one-off printout, but the fix doesn't persist for anyone else who opens the file.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Preview on macOS</strong>: Open the PDF, select a page in the thumbnail sidebar, use the rotate buttons in the toolbar, then File → Export. Built into every Mac, no installation needed.
</p>

<h2>Rotation vs. re-scanning</h2>
<p>
  It's worth being explicit about this because people sometimes assume a sideways scan means starting over: rotating a PDF page never loses information the original scan captured. The pixels or vector content are identical before and after — only the orientation flag or page transform changes. Re-scanning is only necessary if the original page was physically cut off, out of focus, or otherwise poorly captured — not because it's facing the wrong way.
</p>

